<p>The decade began with prices anchored at ${d1970['min']:.2f}/bbl, a relic of the post-colonial pricing regime dominated by Western oil companies. The 1973 OPEC embargo shattered this equilibrium, tripling prices virtually overnight. The 1979 Iranian Revolution drove a second wave of increases, pushing the decade's maximum to ${d1970['max']:.2f}/bbl. The average price of ${d1970['mean']:.2f}/bbl and a CV of {d1970['cv']:.1f}% reflect a decade defined by supply-side shocks that fundamentally restructured the global energy order.</p>

<h3>1980s: Price Collapse and Stabilization</h3>
<p>After peaking in the early 1980s, prices collapsed as demand destruction (from recession and conservation) met surging non-OPEC supply. Saudi Arabia's 1986 decision to defend market share rather than price sent crude from double digits to single digits within months. The decade averaged ${d1980['mean']:.2f}/bbl — higher than the 1970s in absolute terms, but with a declining trend. CV of {d1980['cv']:.1f}% reflects the initial spike followed by sustained decline.</p>

<h3>1990s: The Low-Price Equilibrium</h3>
<p>The 1990s represented a return to relative stability, with prices averaging ${d1990['mean']:.2f}/bbl and a CV of just {d1990['cv']:.1f}% — the lowest of any decade in the dataset. The Gulf War produced a brief spike to ${d1990['max']:.2f}/bbl in 1990, but prices quickly retreated as the conflict's impact on supply proved transient. The Asian Financial Crisis (1997–1998) depressed demand and pushed prices to the decade's low of ${d1990['min']:.2f}/bbl.</p>

<h3>2000s: The Commodity Super-Cycle</h3>
<p>Driven by surging Chinese demand, tightening spare capacity, and financial inflows into commodity markets, prices embarked on a historic rally. The decade average of ${d2000['mean']:.2f}/bbl was more than double the 1990s, with the peak of ${d2000['max']:.2f}/bbl reached in mid-2008. The subsequent crash to ${d2000['min']:.2f}/bbl by year-end 2008 — a decline of over $100 in six months — remains the most dramatic price reversal in the dataset. The CV of {d2000['cv']:.1f}% is the highest of any decade, reflecting extreme dispersion.</p>

<h3>2010s: Shale Revolution and OPEC Strategy Shift</h3>
