<!-- SECTION 6: OUTLOOK & CONCLUSIONS -->
<h2 id="outlook-and-conclusions">6. Outlook &amp; Conclusions</h2>

<h3>Key Takeaways</h3>
<p><strong>1. Structural breaks define oil price history.</strong> The oil market does not revert to a stable equilibrium — it moves between regimes. The 1973 embargo, the 1986 price war, the 2008 crisis, and the 2020 pandemic each represented fundamental shifts in the supply-demand-institutional architecture, not temporary deviations from a mean.</p>

<p><strong>2. Volatility is endemic and clustering.</strong> The standard deviation of ${std_dev:.2f}/bbl over the full period understates the true risk, because volatility clusters: the largest MoM moves are concentrated in crisis windows, while intervening periods show relative calm. Risk models that assume constant volatility will systematically understate tail risk.</p>

<p><strong>3. The super-cycle era may have permanently raised the price floor.</strong> Even after the 2008 crash and the 2014–2016 shale-driven collapse, prices never returned to the sub-$20/bbl levels common before 2000. The structural cost of marginal supply (deepwater, shale, tar sands) has risen, establishing a higher equilibrium range.</p>

<p><strong>4. Geopolitics remains the dominant short-term driver.</strong> Every major price spike in the dataset corresponds to a geopolitical event (embargo, revolution, war, sanctions). Demand-side shocks (2008 financial crisis, 2020 pandemic) produce sharp but typically shorter-lived dislocations.</p>

<p><strong>5. The 2020s are shaping up as a period of elevated uncertainty.</strong> With the energy transition accelerating, OPEC+ managing output more actively, and geopolitical risk elevated (Russia-Ukraine, Middle East), the range of plausible price outcomes is wider than at any point in the dataset's history.</p>

<h3>Recent Trends (2020s)</h3>
<p>The current decade has already produced both the dataset's lowest price point (${d2020['min']:.2f}/bbl, COVID-19) and a sharp recovery driven by supply constraints and geopolitical risk. The average price of ${d2020['mean']:.2f}/bbl through the available observations is the second-highest of any decade after the 2010s, suggesting a new, elevated equilibrium. The wide range (${d2020['range']:.2f}) underscores the persistent uncertainty.</p>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Note on Forward-Looking Data:</strong> Observations from late 2025 through 2026 may represent projections or estimates rather than realized prices. Analysts should treat these data points with appropriate caution and verify against live market data when making forward-looking assessments.
</div>

<h3>Concluding Observations</h3>
<p>Over 56 years, crude oil has proven to be one of the most volatile and geopolitically sensitive commodities in the global economy. The long-term CAGR of {cagr * 100:.2f}% masks the reality that price changes have been neither smooth nor predictable — they have been driven by wars, embargoes, technological revolutions (shale), financial crises, and pandemics. For analysts, the central lesson is that <strong>context matters more than trendlines</strong>: understanding the institutional and geopolitical forces behind price movements is essential for interpreting the data and anticipating future shifts.</p>

