<!-- SECTION 1: EXECUTIVE OVERVIEW -->
<h2 id="executive-overview">1. Executive Overview</h2>
<p>This report analyzes {n} monthly observations of global crude oil prices from January 1970 through {rows[-1]['date'][:7]}, spanning more than five decades of energy market history. The data reveals a commodity that has undergone profound structural transformations — from the fixed-price regime of the early 1970s, through the seismic shocks of OPEC embargoes and gulf wars, to the financialization and shale-driven volatility of the 21st century.</p>

<div class="highlight">
  <strong>Key Findings:</strong> Crude oil prices ranged from ${min_price:.2f}/bbl to ${max_price:.2f}/bbl over the full period, a range of ${price_range:.2f}. The compound annual growth rate from 1970 to {last_year} stands at {cagr * 100:.2f}%, though this headline figure masks enormous cyclical variation. The {highest_avg_decade['decade']} recorded the highest average monthly price at ${highest_avg_decade['mean']:.2f}/bbl, while the largest single-month price swing was ${abs(max_mom_abs['abs_change']):.2f}/bbl ({max_mom_abs['to_date'][:7]}).
</div>

<p>The overall trajectory is one of <strong>punctuated equilibrium</strong>: long stretches of relative stability interrupted by violent dislocations tied to geopolitical conflict, supply disruptions, and demand collapses. Prices in the early 1970s hovered below $3/bbl before the first oil shock catapulted them above $10; by 2008, they briefly surpassed $130 before crashing during the financial crisis; and in 2020, the COVID-19 demand shock drove prices to historic lows before a sharp recovery. Understanding these dynamics is essential for any analyst seeking to contextualize current market conditions.</p>

